3. Midterm mood swing
 
 By Emily Peck 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Note: Figures compare average daily sentiment readings over the seven days before each election with the five days after. Election Day is excluded; Data: Morning Consult; Chart: Emily Peck/Axios There was an unusually sharp partisan mood shift in the wake of the midterms, according to sentiment data from Morning Consult that was shared exclusively with Axios.

 Why it matters: While big partisan swings in consumer sentiment are typical after a presidential election, they have been much smaller in midterm races.

 The latest numbers show just how tightly Americans' views on the economy are now intertwined with politics. Between the lines: Consumer sentiment was once a telling measure of how people would actually spend their money: Good vibes meant more spending.

 Lately, it's looked more like a political measure. Go deeper 


 
 
